Chapter: FieldCacheTextLit
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A field read twice in one call, with a string literal argument between the two
 reads, must give the same answer both times.

 It did not. The emitter caches a field access in the register it loaded it into,
 so a second read of `b.items` in the same expression reuses that register
 instead of loading again. `emit-text-lit` writes RAX with a hardcoded mov-ri64
 before it calls alloc-temp, and alloc-temp only evicts the register it hands
 out -- so the literal destroyed RAX while the cache went on claiming `b.items`
 was still there. The second read then used RAX, which by then held the address
 of the literal, and `list-length` returned the LITERAL's length word.

 So `list-length [7, 8, 9]` returned 9: the character count of "read-text". No
 crash, no diagnostic, just a wrong number -- and the self-compile could not see
 it, because the compiler's own instance of this shape
 (`find-effect-op-addr (st.effect-op-addrs) "read-text" 0 (list-length
 (st.effect-op-addrs))`) walked off the end of a two-element list and happened to
 find no match, which is the answer it wanted anyway. It only became visible as a
 crash when a program's heap layout put a non-canonical pointer
 after the list.

 The literal must stay LONGER than the list is, or the walk stays in bounds and
 the test passes while broken. "read-text" is nine characters; the list is three.

 The empty-literal line was written as a control and is not one: `""` is still a
 text literal, still writes RAX, and returned 0 -- its own length word. Any
 literal triggers this, so the only honest control is the direct read, which
 never had a literal to cross.

 We say:

Section: Entry

  Box = record {
   items : List Integer
  }

  probe : List Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  probe (xs) (name) (i) (len) = len

  opening : [Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let b = Box { items = [7, 8, 9] }
    in act
      print-line-uni ("literal between two reads: " & show (probe (b.items) "read-text" 0 (list-length (b.items))))
      print-line-uni ("empty literal between them: " & show (probe (b.items) "" 0 (list-length (b.items))))
      print-line-uni ("no literal to cross: " & show (list-length (b.items)))
    end
  end
